WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
You already know that a balance sheet is at one date and an income statement is for a period. A turnover ratio divides one by the other, which is why the balance is averaged: a flow across the year is compared with the balance that was there across the year, not with the balance on the last day of it.
11.1  Why the balance is averaged
Every turnover ratio puts a flow over a balance, and the two are measured differently. Net sales happened across twelve months; accounts receivable is a photograph of 31 December. Dividing one by the other compares a year with a day.
The convention is to average the opening and closing balances. For Orontes's receivables that is 4,144,000 + 3,760,000 = 7,904,000, halved: 7,904,000 ÷ 2 = 3,952,000.
The average is a crude correction and the exam knows it. For a seasonal business the two year-end balances may both be unrepresentative — a toy manufacturer's receivables in January say nothing about its receivables in November — and a monthly average would be better if the data existed. Where an exam question gives only a closing balance, use it and say what you have done.
Figure F511-02 sets out the five ratios.
Figure F511-02. The five turnover ratios
@table
Ratio | Numerator | Denominator | Measures
Accounts receivable turnover | Net credit sales | Average accounts receivable | How often the receivables are collected
Inventory turnover | Cost of goods sold | Average inventory | How often the stock is sold
Accounts payable turnover | Purchases | Average accounts payable | How often the suppliers are paid
Total asset turnover | Net sales | Average total assets | Sales produced per dollar of assets
Fixed asset turnover | Net sales | Average net fixed assets | Sales produced per dollar of plant
@end
Three of the numerators repay attention.
Receivable turnover should use credit sales, not total sales, because cash sales never become receivables. Few companies disclose the split, so net sales is used in practice and the exam accepts it — but if a question gives you the credit proportion, use it.
Inventory turnover uses cost of goods sold, not sales. Inventory is carried at cost, so the numerator must be at cost too. Using sales inflates the ratio by the gross margin and is the commonest error in this chapter.
Payable turnover uses purchases, not cost of goods sold. Purchases are cost of goods sold adjusted for the movement in inventory: what was sold, plus what was added to the shelf, less what was taken from it.
EXAM TRAP   Match the numerator to the balance
Inventory is at cost, so the numerator is cost of goods sold.
Payables arise from purchases, so the numerator is purchases, not cost of goods sold.
Receivables arise from credit sales, so the numerator is credit sales where the question gives them.
