A.	Net sales
B.	Cost of goods sold
C.	Purchases
D.	Gross profit
SC11-2  Why is the denominator of a turnover ratio an average?
A.	To smooth out rounding
B.	Because a flow over a year is being compared with a balance at a date
C.	Because the standards require it
D.	Because the closing balance is unaudited
SC11-3  Using net sales instead of cost of goods sold in inventory turnover will:
A.	understate the ratio.
B.	overstate the ratio by the gross margin.
C.	have no effect.
D.	make the ratio negative.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
11.2  The three working-capital turnovers
Figure F511-03 computes them for Orontes.
Figure F511-03. Orontes Foods, Inc. — working-capital turnovers, 2025
@table
Ratio | Numerator | Average balance | Turnover
Accounts receivable | Net sales 42,000,000 | 3,952,000 | 10.63 times
Inventory | Cost of goods sold 27,300,000 | 2,375,000 | 11.49 times
Accounts payable | Purchases 27,450,000 | 1,975,000 | 13.90 times
@end
The arithmetic. Average receivables are (4,144,000 + 3,760,000) ÷ 2 = 3,952,000, and 42,000,000 ÷ 3,952,000 = 10.63. Average inventory is (2,450,000 + 2,300,000) ÷ 2 = 2,375,000, and 27,300,000 ÷ 2,375,000 = 11.49. Purchases are cost of goods sold plus the increase in inventory: 27,300,000 + 150,000 = 27,450,000, where the 150,000 is 2,450,000 − 2,300,000. Average payables are (2,050,000 + 1,900,000) ÷ 2 = 1,975,000, and 27,450,000 ÷ 1,975,000 = 13.90.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: purchases from cost of goods sold
Step 1. The identity. Opening inventory plus purchases less closing inventory equals cost of goods sold. Rearranged: purchases equal cost of goods sold plus closing inventory less opening inventory.
Step 2. Apply it. 27,300,000 + 2,450,000 = 29,750,000, and 29,750,000 − 2,300,000 = 27,450,000.
Step 3. Sense-check the direction. Inventory rose, so Orontes bought more than it sold: purchases exceed cost of goods sold by exactly the increase, $150,000.
Check. Had inventory fallen, purchases would have been less than cost of goods sold. A candidate who writes purchases equal to cost of goods sold has assumed inventory did not move, and the exam sets the question precisely because inventory did.
The three figures read together say something the liquidity ratios did not. Orontes pays its suppliers faster than it collects from its customers: payables turn 13.90 times against receivables at 10.63. Every day of that difference is a day Orontes finances out of its own money, and Chapter 12 converts the difference into days and dollars.
Inventory turning 11.49 times is brisk for a food manufacturer, and it corroborates the common-size finding of Chapter 2 that inventory is only 7.7 per cent of assets. Nothing is sitting on the shelf.
