11.3  The two asset turnovers
The same idea applied to the whole balance sheet asks how much revenue the company extracts from what it owns.
Total asset turnover is net sales divided by average total assets. For Orontes: average total assets are (31,672,600 + 26,445,000) ÷ 2 = 29,058,800, and 42,000,000 ÷ 29,058,800 = 1.45 times.
Fixed asset turnover is net sales divided by average net property, plant and equipment. Average net PP&E is (14,802,000 + 16,000,000) ÷ 2 = 15,401,000, and 42,000,000 ÷ 15,401,000 = 2.73 times.
Figure F511-04 sets them beside two other kinds of company.
Figure F511-04. Asset turnover across three businesses
@table
Business | Total asset turnover | Why
A manufacturer such as Orontes | About 1.5 | Heavy plant; each dollar of assets supports about a dollar and a half of sales
A distributor such as Barada | About 3.0 | Little plant; assets are stock and receivables, which move
A utility | Below 0.5 | Enormous fixed assets with a regulated revenue
@end
Two readings follow for Orontes.
Total asset turnover of 1.45 is low for a food manufacturer, and the reason is in Chapter 2: 23.1 per cent of the assets are cash, which produces no sales at all. Strip the cash out and the ratio on operating assets would be far higher. An analyst would say so, because the ratio as published understates how hard the operating assets work.
Fixed asset turnover of 2.73 is the figure that describes the factory, and it rose during the year: plant fell $1,198,000 while sales rose 15.4 per cent. The plant is being worked harder, which is consistent with a company whose operating leverage is 4.02.
EXAM TRAP   Cash and asset turnover
Idle cash lowers total asset turnover without any change in operations. Say so when the balance sheet shows a large cash holding.
Fixed asset turnover rises when assets are old and heavily depreciated, because the denominator is net of accumulated depreciation. A high ratio can mean an efficient factory or an exhausted one.
Leased assets that are on the balance sheet are in the denominator; a company that leases and expenses rentals has a smaller denominator and a flattering ratio.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: turnover without the cash
Step 1. Average cash. Cash was $7,302,100 at the end of 2025 and $2,100,000 at the end of 2024, so the average is (7,302,100 + 2,100,000) ÷ 2 = 4,701,050.
Step 2. Average operating assets. 29,058,800 − 4,701,050 = 24,357,750.
Step 3. Turnover on operating assets. 42,000,000 ÷ 24,357,750 = 1.72 times, against 1.45 as published.
Check. Nearly three-tenths of a turn is hidden by the cash balance. Neither figure is wrong: 1.45 is what the shareholders' money produces and 1.72 is what the business produces. An answer that gives both, and says which is which, is a Level C answer.
