11.4  What makes a turnover move
A turnover is a fraction, so it moves when the flow moves, when the balance moves, or when both move at different rates. Figure F511-05 names the usual causes.
Figure F511-05. Why a turnover moves
@table
Ratio | Rises when | Falls when
Receivables | Collection tightens; cash sales grow; a large customer settles early | Terms are extended to win sales; a customer stops paying; sales are made late in the year
Inventory | Stock is cut; demand rises; obsolete stock is written off | Stock is built for a season; goods stop selling; a write-down is deferred
Payables | The company pays faster; a discount is taken | The company stretches suppliers; a dispute delays payment
Total assets | Sales rise; assets are sold or written down | Cash accumulates; a large asset is bought before it produces
Fixed assets | Sales rise; plant depreciates | New plant is commissioned before it produces
@end
Two of those entries are the same event seen from opposite sides, and candidates are regularly caught by them.
Writing off obsolete stock raises inventory turnover. The denominator falls and the numerator is unaffected, so a company that finally clears its dead stock reports an improvement in efficiency. The improvement is in the measure and not in the business.
Buying a new plant lowers fixed asset turnover in the year it is commissioned and raises it afterwards. A falling ratio in a year of heavy capital expenditure is expected and is not a finding.
YOUR TURN   Compute Barada's turnovers
