11.5  Reading the five together
The turnovers corroborate one another and they corroborate the liquidity ratios, and a Level C answer uses the cross-checks.
Receivables turnover of 10.63 against a current ratio of 3.31. The current ratio is high and the receivables are moving, so the high ratio is not uncollected debts. Had receivables turnover been 4 or 5, the same current ratio would have meant something very different.
Inventory turnover of 11.49 against inventory at 7.7 per cent of assets. Both say the same thing: little money is tied up in stock and what is there moves.
Total asset turnover of 1.45 against cash at 23.1 per cent of assets. These point in opposite directions and the cash explains the turnover, which is the finding.
Payables turnover of 13.90 against receivables at 10.63. Orontes funds the gap, and Chapter 12 prices it.
Figure F511-07 lists the pairs worth checking.
Figure F511-07. Cross-checks between the turnovers and the earlier ratios
@table
Turnover | Check it against | What agreement means | What disagreement means
Receivables | The current and quick ratios | Liquidity is real | A high current ratio is uncollected debts
Inventory | Inventory as a share of assets | Stock is under control | Stock is accumulating
Payables | The cash ratio | The company pays from strength | The company is stretching suppliers
Total assets | The common-size balance sheet | Assets are working | Something large is idle
Fixed assets | Capital expenditure in the cash flow statement | Capacity matches sales | New capacity is not yet producing
@end
