Chapter summary
Figure F511-08 brings the chapter together.
Figure F511-08. Chapter 11 summary map: the key rule for each section.
A turnover divides a flow by the average balance that produced it and answers in times a year. The numerator must match the balance: cost of goods sold against inventory because inventory is at cost, purchases against payables because payables arise from purchases, credit sales against receivables. Purchases are cost of goods sold plus the increase in inventory, which for Orontes is 27,300,000 + 150,000 = 27,450,000. Orontes collects 10.63 times, sells its stock 11.49 times and pays its suppliers 13.90 times, so it pays faster than it collects and finances the difference. Total asset turnover is 1.45 and fixed asset turnover 2.73; the first is held down by cash at 23.1 per cent of assets, and on operating assets alone it would be 1.72. A turnover moves when the flow moves, the balance moves, or both move at different rates, and two cases catch candidates: writing off dead stock improves inventory turnover without improving anything, and new plant depresses fixed asset turnover in the year it is commissioned. Finally, the turnovers cross-check the liquidity ratios: a high current ratio with slow receivables means something quite different from a high current ratio with fast ones.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P11-01  Accounts payable turnover uses which numerator?
A.	Cost of goods sold
B.	Purchases
C.	Net sales
D.	Operating expenses
P11-02  Cost of goods sold is 27,300,000, closing inventory is 2,450,000 and opening inventory is 2,300,000. Purchases are:
A.	27,150,000
B.	27,450,000
C.	27,300,000
D.	29,750,000
P11-03  Average inventory is 2,375,000 and cost of goods sold is 27,300,000. Inventory turnover is:
A.	11.49
B.	17.68
C.	10.63
D.	13.90
P11-04  Average total assets are 29,058,800 and net sales are 42,000,000. Total asset turnover is:
A.	0.69
B.	1.45
C.	2.73
D.	1.33
P11-05  Which event raises inventory turnover without improving the business?
A.	A rise in sales
B.	Writing off obsolete stock
C.	A fall in purchase prices
D.	A new warehouse
P11-06  Fixed asset turnover may be high because the plant is efficient or because:
A.	the plant is new.
B.	the plant is old and heavily depreciated.
C.	sales are understated.
D.	the company owns its buildings.
P11-07  A company's receivables turnover falls from 10 to 6 while sales are unchanged. The most likely cause is:
A.	faster collection.
B.	extended credit terms or slower payment by customers.
C.	more cash sales.
D.	a write-off of bad debts.
P11-08  Orontes's total asset turnover would be 1.72 rather than 1.45 if:
A.	the cash balance were excluded from the denominator.
B.	the fixed assets were excluded.
C.	sales were measured gross.
D.	the closing balance were used instead of the average.
P11-09  Which pair would most worry an analyst?
A.	Current ratio 3.3, receivables turnover 10.6
B.	Current ratio 3.3, receivables turnover 4.0
C.	Current ratio 1.2, receivables turnover 12.0
D.	Current ratio 2.0, inventory turnover 11.0
P11-10  Orontes pays its suppliers 13.90 times a year and collects 10.63 times. This means Orontes:
A.	is funded by its suppliers.
B.	funds the gap between paying and collecting.
C.	has a negative cash cycle.
D.	has no inventory.
Case-style practice: Barada's turnovers against Orontes's
Barada Wholesale's receivables turnover is 12.01 times, its inventory turnover is 9.00 times and its payables turnover is 13.70 times. Orontes's are 10.63, 11.49 and 13.90.
Required. Say which company collects faster, which sells its stock faster, and which pays its suppliers faster. Then explain why a distributor would be expected to differ from a manufacturer on at least one of the three, and name the one further figure you would want before concluding that either company is better managed. Under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC11-1  Answer B. Inventory is carried at cost, so the numerator must be at cost.
A is wrong. Net sales includes the gross margin and inflates the ratio.
C is wrong. Purchases is the numerator for payables.
D is wrong. Gross profit is not a cost flow through inventory.
SC11-2  Answer B. A flow across twelve months is compared with a balance at one date.
A is wrong. Rounding is not the issue.
C is wrong. The standards do not prescribe analytical ratios.
D is wrong. Both balances come from audited statements.
SC11-3  Answer B. Sales exceed cost by the gross margin, so the ratio is inflated.
A is wrong. A larger numerator raises the ratio.
C is wrong. The numerator changes.
D is wrong. Both figures are positive.
SC11-4  Answer B. Purchases equal cost of goods sold plus the increase in inventory.
A is wrong. This reverses the sign.
C is wrong. This ignores the opening balance.
D is wrong. Gross profit does not enter the identity.
SC11-5  Answer B. A higher payables turnover means a shorter payment period.
A is wrong. Collection is slower than payment.
C is wrong. The gap is exactly the working capital requirement.
D is wrong. Supplier funding would show as a lower payables turnover.
SC11-6  Answer A. 27,300,000 ÷ 2,375,000 = 11.49.
B is wrong. This uses net sales as the numerator.
C is wrong. 10.63 is receivables turnover.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
SC11-7  Answer B. Total asset turnover uses average total assets.
A is wrong. A year-end balance compares a year with a day.
C is wrong. Operating assets give a different, unpublished measure.
D is wrong. Net fixed assets is the denominator of fixed asset turnover.
SC11-8  Answer B. Cash produces no sales, so it enlarges the denominator and lowers the ratio.
A is wrong. A larger denominator lowers the ratio.
C is wrong. Cash is part of total assets.
D is wrong. The ratio remains calculable.
SC11-9  Answer B. The denominator is net of accumulated depreciation, so old plant gives a small denominator.
A is wrong. New plant raises the denominator and lowers the ratio.
C is wrong. Understated sales would lower the ratio.
D is wrong. Owning rather than leasing raises the denominator.
SC11-10  Answer B. The average balance falls and cost of goods sold is unchanged.
A is wrong. A smaller denominator raises the ratio.
C is wrong. The denominator changes.
D is wrong. Both figures remain positive.
SC11-11  Answer B. The asset is in the denominator before it generates sales.
A is wrong. Sales need not fall.
C is wrong. Depreciation reduces the denominator and would raise the ratio.
D is wrong. A leased asset on the balance sheet has the same effect.
SC11-12  Answer B. Longer terms mean a larger receivables balance for the same sales.
A is wrong. A larger denominator lowers the ratio.
C is wrong. The balance changes.
D is wrong. The effect persists while the terms do.
SC11-13  Answer B. Slow collection inflates receivables, which inflates current assets.
A is wrong. Cash would not depress receivables turnover.
C is wrong. Rapid inventory turnover reduces inventory.
D is wrong. Low current liabilities would raise the ratio without affecting turnover; but the turnover figure given points to receivables.
SC11-14  Answer B. Cash produces no sales and is nearly a quarter of the denominator.
A is wrong. Receivables turn 10.63 times, which is not slow.
C is wrong. Inventory is 7.7 per cent of assets.
D is wrong. Plant fell during the year.
