@table
Item | Answer
Accounts receivable turnover | 12.01 times: 21,978,000 ÷ 1,830,000.
Inventory turnover | 9.00 times: 18,900,000 ÷ 2,100,000.
Accounts payable turnover | 13.70 times: 19,180,000 ÷ 1,400,000.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P11-01  Answer B. Payables arise from purchases.
A is wrong. Cost of goods sold is the inventory numerator.
C is wrong. Net sales is the receivables and asset numerator.
D is wrong. Operating expenses are not bought on trade credit in this sense.
P11-02  Answer B. 27,300,000 + 2,450,000 − 2,300,000 = 27,450,000.
A is wrong. This subtracts the increase instead of adding it.
C is wrong. This assumes inventory did not move.
D is wrong. This omits the opening inventory deduction.
P11-03  Answer A. 27,300,000 ÷ 2,375,000 = 11.49.
B is wrong. This uses net sales.
C is wrong. 10.63 is receivables turnover.
D is wrong. 13.90 is payables turnover.
P11-04  Answer B. 42,000,000 ÷ 29,058,800 = 1.45.
A is wrong. This is the reciprocal.
C is wrong. 2.73 is fixed asset turnover.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
P11-05  Answer B. The average balance falls and the cost flow is unchanged.
A is wrong. A rise in sales is a real improvement.
C is wrong. Lower purchase prices reduce the numerator as well.
D is wrong. A warehouse is a fixed asset.
P11-06  Answer B. The denominator is net of accumulated depreciation.
A is wrong. New plant lowers the ratio.
C is wrong. Understated sales lower the ratio.
D is wrong. Owning raises the denominator.
P11-07  Answer B. The balance grows for the same sales.
A is wrong. Faster collection raises the ratio.
C is wrong. More cash sales reduce receivables and raise the ratio.
D is wrong. A write-off reduces receivables and raises the ratio.
P11-08  Answer A. Removing average cash of 4,701,050 from the denominator gives 1.72.
B is wrong. Excluding fixed assets would raise the ratio far above 1.72.
C is wrong. Sales are already net.
D is wrong. The closing balance would lower the ratio.
P11-09  Answer B. A high current ratio built on slow receivables is the dangerous combination.
A is wrong. Both figures are healthy and consistent.
C is wrong. Fast collection supports a low current ratio.
D is wrong. Both figures are unremarkable.
P11-10  Answer B. Paying faster than collecting means the company bridges the difference.
A is wrong. Supplier funding would require the opposite.
C is wrong. A negative cash cycle requires payables to be slower than receivables and inventory combined.
D is wrong. Orontes holds $2,450,000 of inventory.
Case-style set: Barada's turnovers against Orontes's
Barada collects faster: 12.01 times against Orontes's 10.63. Orontes sells its stock faster: 11.49 times against Barada's 9.00. Orontes pays its suppliers marginally faster: 13.90 against 13.70, which on any view is a difference too small to carry an argument.
A distributor would be expected to differ on inventory. Barada buys finished goods and holds them until a retailer orders; it earns a thin margin and its whole business is the stockroom, with inventory at 34 per cent of its assets against Orontes's 7.7. A manufacturer converts material continuously and holds stock only as long as the process needs. So a distributor turning its stock 9.00 times against a manufacturer's 11.49 is not evidence of worse management; it is evidence of a different business.
The collection difference is harder to explain away, because both sell to the same kind of customer. Barada collecting in about 30 days against Orontes's 34 suggests either tighter terms or a different customer mix.
The further figure I would want is the ageing of each company's receivables. Turnover is an average, and an average of 12.01 is consistent with every customer paying in 30 days or with most paying in 20 and one large one paying in 120. Which of those is true changes the judgment about both companies entirely, and no turnover ratio can distinguish them.
